    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Wait, what? I'm not sure I follow the goblin part. Can you just stick to the technical side? I'm trying to understand the actual physics. Also, you mentioned pressure differences—what happens if that tube gets blocked by something, like an insect or debris? How does that affect the reading?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I got a bit carried away with the goblin analogy. Let's focus on the technical side.\n\nThe pitot tube measures airspeed by detecting the dynamic pressure of the air flowing over it. Dynamic pressure is the pressure exerted by a fluid (in this case, air) due to its velocity. The pitot tube is designed to be sensitive to changes in airspeed, and it does this by creating a pressure difference between the front and back of the tube.\n\nWhen air flows over the pitot tube, it creates a region of lower pressure above the tube and a region of higher pressure below it. This pressure difference is proportional to the airspeed. The airspeed indicator then uses this pressure difference to calculate the airspeed of the aircraft.\n\nNow, if the pitot tube becomes blocked by debris or an insect, the pressure difference is disrupted, and the airspeed reading becomes inaccurate. This is because the blockage prevents the air from flowing freely over the pitot tube, resulting in a reduced pressure difference. As a result, the airspeed indicator may display an incorrect airspeed reading.\n\nIn extreme cases, a blocked pitot tube can cause the airspeed indicator to become stuck or to display a false reading. This can be a serious",
      "source": "target",
